## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical debugging across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and Redis in a realistic CNPA flow, focusing on end-to-end latency and data correctness during policy changes.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency measurement across services
- Distributed tracing and per-tenant sampling
- Kafka producer/consumer offset lag and acks
- Transactional outbox and idempotent sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and read-after-write verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo transactional outbox
db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.insert('audit', auditRec);
  await tx.insert('outbox', { ...auditRec, event: 'AUDIT_LOG' });
  // commit happens here
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you distinguish tenant-specific lag from global system lag?
- What config changes to Kafka/SRE tooling help monitor and mitigate this without harming durability?

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced multi-region CNPA scenarios demand precise fault-localization across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and cross-region replication. This question probes systematic debugging, observability design, and practical fixes that preserve data integrity and latency.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-region Kafka replication and lag diagnosis
- End-to-end tracing across services
- Producer batching, acks, and idempotent writes
- Failover testing and impact assessment

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: add a tracing span around HTTP handler to trace end-to-end latency
const tracer = require('@opentelemetry/api');

app.post('/api/events', async (req, res) => {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http.handle-events');
  try {
    // Business logic here
    await processEvent(req.body);
    res.status(200).json({ success: true });
  } catch (error) {
    span.recordException(error);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Processing failed' });
  } finally {
    span.end();
  }
});
```

